    "narrative_title": "Global interest-rate shock and market repricing",
    "narrative_summary": "Major central banks are tightening policy or signaling higher-for-longer rates, triggering a broad repricing across bonds, equities, FX and credit. As an open system, policy decisions, market expectations and cross-border capital flows exchange information and resources with firms, households and investors through reinforcing and balancing feedbacks: higher policy rates lift yields, tighten credit and depress risk assets, which in turn alter inflation and growth expectations and feed back into policy. External actors and shocks (commodity supply, fiscal moves, China’s Treasury flows, FX moves) act as inputs that propagate through interconnected markets, creating contagion and adjustment dynamics.",
    "scope": "Central-bank policy (rates, guidance, balance sheets), government bond markets and yields, equity markets and investor positioning, FX and international capital flows, credit and corporate financing conditions, housing/mortgages, major market participants (banks, asset managers, sovereign holders), and inflation/expectations dynamics.",
